recipe pty: run `ls --color=always` in a pty, read until eof, feed a Grid, print the screen as text

Run a program on a pseudo-terminal and read what it drew: `pty.spawn` starts it with a size, `tryRead`
collects its bytes, a `Grid` plus `Parser` turn them into cells. Here `ls --color=always` runs and `dumpText` prints the screen as text.
`j3c doc term` is the overview; this is the emulator half without a window.

- Linux, and Windows 10 1809+ (ConPTY; run `cmd.exe` or `powershell.exe` there, `\r` ends a line). `spawn` is `Err` when the program cannot start.
- Read with `p.waitReadable(ms)` plus `tryRead` and a deadline (the same on every platform; a reader thread blocks in it), never a bare loop: a program that never exits would hang you. `eof` is true once it closed its side.
- `p.write(bytes)` sends keys to it (queued, flushed on the next `tryRead`), `p.resize(cols, rows)` changes its size.
- `p.wait()` asks it to quit (SIGHUP, then SIGKILL after N ms; on Windows the console closes, then it is terminated) and returns its exit code. `j3.term.view.View` draws a Grid in a window (`examples/terminal`).

==> j3.toml <==
[package]
name = "ptydemo"
entry = "src/main.j3"

==> src/main.j3 <==
module ptydemo;

use j3.io.printLn;
use j3.memory.Arena;
use j3.result.Result;
use j3.term.ansi.Parser;
use j3.term.ansi.feed;
use j3.term.grid.Grid;
use j3.term.pty;

fn main() {
    let a = Arena.create(4194304);
    let buf = a.alloc::<u8>(4096);
    let none: [1]string = [""];
    let argv: [3]string = ["ls", "--color=always", "."];
    match pty.spawn(argv[:], "", none[0..0], 80, 24, &a) {
        Result.Ok(p) {
            let grid = Grid.new(&a, 80, 24, 100);
            let parser = Parser.new(&a);
            // read until the program closes its side, or about N s
            for i in 0..100 {
                p.waitReadable(50);
                parser.feed(p.tryRead(buf), &grid);
                if p.eof { break; }
            }
            printLn(grid.dumpText(&a));
            p.wait();
            p.close();
        }
        Result.Err(_) { printLn("could not spawn ls"); }
    }
}
